WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Welcome. Responsible AI use begins before
you type a prompt

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.000
and continues after a result reaches a
person. In this

3
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:18.000
class, you will set a data boundary, scale
safeguards to

4
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:24.000
consequence, separate privacy from
security, fairness, transparency, and

5
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:30.000
accountability, and build a one-page
responsible-use plan. The goal is

6
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.000
practical judgment. You should finish able
to pause an unsafe

7
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:42.000
request, redesign a risky workflow, and
name the person responsible

8
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:48.000
for monitoring and recovery.

9
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.000
Use a pause-before-paste rule. First,
identify the information. Then ask

10
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:58.000
whether you are authorized to use it for
this purpose.

11
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:03.000
Confirm which product, account, and
workspace are approved. Learn what

12
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:08.000
the service retains, who can access the
content, whether it

13
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:13.000
may be used for improvement, and how
deletion works. Sensitive

14
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:18.000
material includes personal, health,
financial, employment, education,

15
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:23.000
customer, and regulated records. It also
includes credentials, private keys,

16
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:28.000
confidential source code, unreleased
plans, contracts, and legal advice.

17
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:33.000
Ordinary facts can become identifying when
combined. A name, work

18
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:38.000
location, schedule, and unusual event may
point to one person

19
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:43.000
even when no single field looks secret.
Minimize the input

20
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:48.000
before prompting. Remove fields the task
does not need. Replace

21
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:53.000
real details with representative values
when the result does not

22
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:58.000
depend on identity. Use approved retrieval
or controlled data connections

23
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:03.000
when copying would weaken governance.
Never place passwords, tokens, or

24
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:08.000
private keys in prompts, examples,
screenshots, or transcripts. If policy,

25
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:13.000
authorization, retention, or audience is
unknown, stop and resolve the

26
00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:18.000
boundary before continuing.

27
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.667
Imagine that a manager wants AI to rewrite
performance feedback.

28
00:02:22.667 --> 00:02:27.333
The original note contains the employee's
name, disability accommodation, salary,

29
00:02:27.333 --> 00:02:32.000
customer complaint, and a private access
token copied from a

30
00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:36.667
troubleshooting log. The deliverable is a
clearer paragraph about observable

31
00:02:36.667 --> 00:02:41.333
work behavior. The name is not needed for
drafting. The

32
00:02:41.333 --> 00:02:46.000
accommodation and salary are sensitive and
outside the writing purpose.

33
00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:50.667
The customer detail should be generalized
unless an approved reviewer

34
00:02:50.667 --> 00:02:55.333
needs exact evidence. The token is a
secret and must

35
00:02:55.333 --> 00:03:00.000
never enter the system; it also requires
rotation if it

36
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.667
was exposed elsewhere. The safer input
uses a fictional role,

37
00:03:04.667 --> 00:03:09.333
removes unrelated personal facts, replaces
the customer with a neutral

38
00:03:09.333 --> 00:03:14.000
description, and states the permitted tone
and decision boundary. The

39
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:18.667
manager still owns the final review.
Redaction is not permission.

40
00:03:18.667 --> 00:03:23.333
Even a minimized prompt must use an
approved product and

41
00:03:23.333 --> 00:03:28.000
an authorized purpose.

42
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:33.000
Choose one document or data set you might
use with

43
00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:38.000
AI. Without revealing its contents,
classify the information as allowed,

44
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:43.000
restricted, prohibited, or unknown. Name
the minimum fields the task

45
00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:48.000
requires. If any authorization or product
rule is unknown, record

46
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:53.000
that as a stop condition.

47
00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:59.000
Checkpoint. A team removes names from
customer support messages and

48
00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:05.000
wants to paste the full history into a
free public

49
00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:11.000
AI account. They do not know the
provider's retention settings

50
00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:17.000
or whether combinations of dates,
locations, and rare incidents can

51
00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:23.000
identify customers. Is removing names
enough to proceed?

52
00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:28.000
Stop. Removing names does not establish
that the messages are

53
00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:33.000
anonymous, permitted, or protected.
Combined facts may identify people. The

54
00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:38.000
team must confirm authorization, approved
service and account, retention, access,

55
00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:43.000
training or improvement use, deletion,
contractual terms, and the minimum

56
00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:48.000
content needed. A safer design may
aggregate categories, use synthetic

57
00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:53.000
examples, run an approved controlled
service, or avoid the upload.

58
00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:58.000
A disclaimer does not repair an
unauthorized disclosure. If you

59
00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:03.000
chose to proceed because the task seems
helpful, revise the

60
00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:08.000
rule: expected benefit never substitutes
for permission and data governance.

61
00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:13.625
Risk depends on the use case, not only the

62
00:07:13.625 --> 00:07:19.250
model. Compare a private list of picnic
ideas with a

63
00:07:19.250 --> 00:07:24.875
system that ranks job applicants. The
first result is easy

64
00:07:24.875 --> 00:07:30.500
to inspect, affects few people, and can be
discarded. The

65
00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:36.125
second can shape access to employment, may
affect many people,

66
00:07:36.125 --> 00:07:41.750
can reproduce historical disadvantage, and
may be difficult for an

67
00:07:41.750 --> 00:07:47.375
applicant to detect or correct. Evaluate
consequence, exposure, reversibility,

68
00:07:47.375 --> 00:07:53.000
affected people, data sensitivity,
uncertainty, and the system's power to

69
00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:58.625
act. Increase evidence, testing, access
control, qualified review, monitoring,

70
00:07:58.625 --> 00:08:04.250
documentation, and recovery as those
factors rise. Keep AI assistance

71
00:08:04.250 --> 00:08:09.875
away from the final decision when reliable
oversight cannot be

72
00:08:09.875 --> 00:08:15.500
demonstrated. Some uses should not launch
because the residual harm

73
00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:21.125
remains unacceptable. Human review is not
magic. A reviewer needs

74
00:08:21.125 --> 00:08:26.750
time, authority, relevant expertise,
evidence, and a real ability to

75
00:08:26.750 --> 00:08:32.375
reject or correct the output. A
rubber-stamp approval adds ceremony

76
00:08:32.375 --> 00:08:38.000
without control.

77
00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:43.882
Responsible use requires several distinct
questions. Privacy asks whether data

78
00:08:43.882 --> 00:08:49.765
collection, use, retention, sharing,
export, and deletion are appropriate.

79
00:08:49.765 --> 00:08:55.647
Security asks whether unauthorized people
or instructions can access data,

80
00:08:55.647 --> 00:09:01.529
change behavior, misuse tools, or escape
controls. Safety asks what

81
00:09:01.529 --> 00:09:07.412
harms can occur during intended use,
foreseeable misuse, or failure.

82
00:09:07.412 --> 00:09:13.294
Fairness asks whether error rates, access,
burdens, or outcomes differ

83
00:09:13.294 --> 00:09:19.176
unacceptably across people and situations.
Transparency asks whether users and

84
00:09:19.176 --> 00:09:25.059
affected people understand that AI is
involved, what role it

85
00:09:25.059 --> 00:09:30.941
plays, which limits matter, and how to
question an outcome.

86
00:09:30.941 --> 00:09:36.824
Accountability names the people and
organization that approve, operate,

87
00:09:36.824 --> 00:09:42.706
monitor, investigate, correct, and retire
the workflow. A system can

88
00:09:42.706 --> 00:09:48.588
be strong in one dimension and weak in
another. Encryption

89
00:09:48.588 --> 00:09:54.471
does not prove fairness. A fairness
benchmark does not prevent

90
00:09:54.471 --> 00:10:00.353
credential theft. A disclosure banner does
not create an appeal

91
00:10:00.353 --> 00:10:06.235
path. Content filtering does not establish
factual accuracy. Treat these

92
00:10:06.235 --> 00:10:12.118
dimensions as connected lenses, then
translate each material concern into

93
00:10:12.118 --> 00:10:18.000
an owner, test, control, and recovery
path.

94
00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:25.000
Checkpoint. An AI-supported benefits
workflow encrypts its data, restricts

95
00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:32.000
administrator access, and records every
request. Affected employees are not

96
00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:39.000
told AI is involved. They receive no
explanation, correction path,

97
00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:46.000
or named decision owner. Which dimensions
remain materially weak?

98
00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:51.000
Transparency and accountability are
clearly weak. People need to understand

99
00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:56.000
AI's role and important limitations. They
need a usable path

100
00:11:56.000 --> 00:12:01.000
to ask questions, correct records, appeal
an outcome, and reach

101
00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:06.000
an accountable owner. The security
controls are valuable, but they

102
00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:11.000
do not answer whether the workflow is
accurate or fair.

103
00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:16.000
Those claims require representative
evaluation and impact evidence. If you

104
00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:21.000
answered privacy alone, separate data
handling from notice and recourse.

105
00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:26.000
If you answered that encryption makes the
system responsible, remember

106
00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:31.000
that one strong dimension cannot
substitute for the others.

107
00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:36.556
Build controls around the whole workflow.
Start with purpose, owner,

108
00:12:36.556 --> 00:12:42.111
intended users, affected people, approved
data, prohibited data, expected behavior,

109
00:12:42.111 --> 00:12:47.667
and excluded decisions. Map plausible
misuse and failure. Define launch

110
00:12:47.667 --> 00:12:53.222
criteria and tests using representative
cases, including difficult and adversarial

111
00:12:53.222 --> 00:12:58.778
examples. Prevention controls can minimize
data, scope credentials, allowlist tools,

112
00:12:58.778 --> 00:13:04.333
filter inputs, validate formats,
rate-limit use, and require approval

113
00:13:04.333 --> 00:13:09.889
before consequential actions. Detection
controls can compare outputs to evidence,

114
00:13:09.889 --> 00:13:15.444
monitor quality and disparity metrics,
record audit events, detect unusual

115
00:13:15.444 --> 00:13:21.000
access, and collect user reports. Response
controls assign triage, notification,

116
00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:26.556
correction, containment, and escalation.
Recovery controls restore records, roll

117
00:13:26.556 --> 00:13:32.111
back releases, revoke access, provide
appeal, and retire an unsafe

118
00:13:32.111 --> 00:13:37.667
feature. Google describes policy,
evaluation, and safeguards as layers; it

119
00:13:37.667 --> 00:13:43.222
also warns that classifiers can produce
false positives and false

120
00:13:43.222 --> 00:13:48.778
negatives. Measure the safeguards
themselves. Document residual risk and who

121
00:13:48.778 --> 00:13:54.333
accepted it. Monitor after launch because
data, models, users, attacks,

122
00:13:54.333 --> 00:13:59.889
and operating conditions change. Set a
re-evaluation trigger, a stop

123
00:13:59.889 --> 00:14:05.444
condition, and a retirement owner before
the first real user

124
00:14:05.444 --> 00:14:11.000
is affected.

125
00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:16.000
Open the responsible-use plan activity.
Choose one realistic AI-assisted use

126
00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:21.000
case. State its purpose, named owner,
intended users, affected people,

127
00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:26.000
and excluded decisions. List allowed,
restricted, prohibited, and unknown input

128
00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:31.000
data. Describe plausible failures and rate
their consequence, exposure, and

129
00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:36.000
reversibility. For every high-severity
risk, add at least one preventive

130
00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:41.000
control and one detection or recovery
control. Define verification, qualified

131
00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:46.000
human approval, permissions, monitoring,
incident response, correction, appeal

132
00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:51.000
where relevant, and retirement. Finish
with one condition that blocks

133
00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:56.000
launch and one metric or event that
triggers re-evaluation. If

134
00:14:56.000 --> 00:15:01.000
you cannot name an owner or recovery path,
the plan

135
00:15:01.000 --> 00:15:06.000
is not ready.

136
00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:10.286
When you are ready, begin the knowledge
check. You will

137
00:18:10.286 --> 00:18:14.571
decide when minimization occurs, scale
safeguards to consequence, identify

138
00:18:14.571 --> 00:18:18.857
missing transparency and accountability,
choose layered controls for important

139
00:18:18.857 --> 00:18:23.143
records, and assign responsibility to
people and organizations rather than

140
00:18:23.143 --> 00:18:27.429
a model. Review the class or return to
your plan

141
00:18:27.429 --> 00:18:31.714
before submitting. The assessment begins
only when you choose Begin

142
00:18:31.714 --> 00:18:36.000
knowledge check.

143
00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:42.667
Pause before data crosses the boundary.
Scale controls to consequence.

144
00:18:42.667 --> 00:18:49.333
Test every responsibility dimension. Keep
an accountable person able to

145
00:18:49.333 --> 00:18:56.000
stop, correct, recover, and retire the
workflow.
